WEBVTT

00:00:05.643 --> 00:00:10.013
<v><b>Speaker:</b></v> I’d like you to look at the images and think about what jobs you can see.

00:00:23.576 --> 00:00:29.422
You’re almost certainly thinking ‘postman’, ‘fireman’, ‘policeman’.

00:00:29.858 --> 00:00:31.987
The English language is evolving.

00:00:32.447 --> 00:00:39.143
Many words and expressions that were in regular use are now no longer acceptable in modern society.

00:00:39.795 --> 00:00:44.770
They reflect the male-dominated social structures of past eras, and,

00:00:44.795 --> 00:00:47.559
as a result, they discriminate against women.

00:00:48.281 --> 00:00:55.740
The trend nowadays is towards gender-neutrality, that is, to use language which includes everybody.

00:00:56.897 --> 00:01:01.008
Here are five ways in which you can make your language gender-neutral.

00:01:01.778 --> 00:01:06.278
Firstly, it’s best to use a neutral word if possible.

00:01:06.897 --> 00:01:11.057
The suffix -<i>man</i> was used to describe many social roles

00:01:11.082 --> 00:01:14.954
and occupations because they were traditionally performed by men.

00:01:15.650 --> 00:01:22.633
However, in the 1970s, as women were increasingly taking on more significant social

00:01:22.722 --> 00:01:29.634
and professional roles, people started to replace <i>chairman</i> with <i>chairperson</i> or <i>chair</i>.

00:01:30.499 --> 00:01:36.619
Now jobs, once mainly done by men, are increasingly carried out by either sex

00:01:36.785 --> 00:01:40.511
and the gender-neutral suffix -<i>person</i> is preferred:

00:01:40.980 --> 00:01:47.009
so, <i>spokesperson</i> instead of <i>spokesman</i>, <i>salesperson</i> instead of <i>salesman</i>.

00:01:48.128 --> 00:01:53.945
However, we don’t always automatically substitute -<i>person</i> for -<i>man</i>.

00:01:54.406 --> 00:01:57.975
Sometimes a gender-neutral alternative has evolved.

00:01:58.428 --> 00:02:03.591
For example, we say <i>police officer</i> rather than <i>policeman</i> or <i>policewoman</i>.

00:02:04.805 --> 00:02:08.366
Other examples include <i>fire fighter</i> rather than <i>fireman</i>,

00:02:09.818 --> 00:02:15.698
<i>refuse collector</i> rather than <i>dustman</i>, and <i>flight attendant</i> rather than <i>air hostess</i>.

00:02:18.259 --> 00:02:25.980
A second way to make language gender-neutral is to avoid the suffix -<i>ess</i> – that’s E-S-S.

00:02:26.964 --> 00:02:30.765
This suffix, which has been used since medieval times,

00:02:30.844 --> 00:02:35.061
is added to a male noun to indicate the equivalent role for a woman.

00:02:37.030 --> 00:02:42.863
For example, <i>manager–manageress</i>, <i>actor–actress</i>.

00:02:45.376 --> 00:02:47.473
By the end of the 20th century,

00:02:47.598 --> 00:02:54.144
it was no longer considered relevant to specify whether a job was being done by a man or a woman.

00:02:54.827 --> 00:02:59.810
The female form was seen as patronizing to women and fell out of fashion,

00:03:00.001 --> 00:03:02.685
and the male form became the standard term.

00:03:03.137 --> 00:03:08.309
So now, for example, both men and women are <i>managers</i> or <i>actors</i>.

00:03:09.595 --> 00:03:13.863
Thirdly, don’t say <i>man</i> when you mean <i>everybody</i>.

00:03:15.348 --> 00:03:19.100
This is a less obvious change that it’s easy to forget about.

00:03:19.592 --> 00:03:23.232
In Old English, the word <i>man</i> meant <i>human being</i>,

00:03:23.844 --> 00:03:30.328
but now, to refer to humanity in general, try to avoid the use of <i>man</i> or <i>mankind</i>.

00:03:31.177 --> 00:03:35.959
There are a number of gender-neutral alternatives, depending on the context.

00:03:36.547 --> 00:03:41.989
Talk about <i>the future of the human race</i>, for example, rather than <i>the future of mankind</i>.

00:03:42.663 --> 00:03:48.726
Instead of describing a <i>manpower shortage</i>, refer to a <i>shortage of staff</i> or <i>workers</i>.

00:03:49.544 --> 00:03:54.740
Don’t ask somebody to <i>man</i> the front desk – it can be <i>staffed</i> or <i>run</i>.

00:03:55.810 --> 00:04:03.012
The adjective <i>man-made</i> is easily replaced by <i>synthetic</i> or <i>artificial</i>, whilst the opinion

00:04:03.037 --> 00:04:09.254
of the <i>man in the street</i> is really just the <i>opinion of the average person</i> or <i>ordinary people</i>.

00:04:10.424 --> 00:04:14.658
Another important thing is to use <i>woman</i>, not <i>girl</i>.

00:04:15.149 --> 00:04:22.126
An absolute no-no is to use <i>girl</i> to refer to any woman in a particular job or role.

00:04:22.515 --> 00:04:28.712
This term implies that women are in some way immature, children rather than adults.

00:04:29.077 --> 00:04:36.157
So, for example, while in the past, a man might have referred to the <i>weather girl</i> on morning TV,

00:04:36.451 --> 00:04:40.178
it is much more appropriate now to describe this person <i>as</i>

00:04:40.178 --> 00:04:46.319
‘the woman who presents the weather’ or use the gender-neutral term <i>weather presenter</i>.

00:04:47.644 --> 00:04:51.220
The final piece of advice is to beware of binomials.

00:04:52.379 --> 00:04:59.395
Binomials, phrases consisting of two words joined by a conjunction, such as <i>and</i>,

00:04:59.625 --> 00:05:06.538
typically place the male word before the female, so <i>men and women, boys and girls</i>,

00:05:07.110 --> 00:05:10.575
<i>brothers and sisters</i>, and <i>Mr and Mrs</i>.

00:05:11.456 --> 00:05:17.312
Just remember, you don’t have to put the male word first: mix it up, girls and boys!

00:05:18.724 --> 00:05:23.083
Society is changing and the English language is evolving to match.

00:05:23.551 --> 00:05:26.426
You are still likely to hear gendered language.

00:05:26.752 --> 00:05:33.900
But keep your eyes and ears open, be careful what you say, and go as gender-neutral as you can!